// Oracle questions about `index` and `size` that `eval-forms` does not ask:
// how the *index* is converted, what an out-of-range read answers, and what
// an unspecified byte contributes to a section's raw table.
//
// The conversion is the question that matters.  Every other numeric boundary
// in the evaluator rounds half up, so `index(t, 1.7)` would be `t[2]` if it
// took that rule; `Eval_Index_1D` calls `EvalAny::ToInt` instead
// (compiler_eval.cpp:349, eval.h:28), which is `int(floor(x) + .5f)` and
// lands on `t[1]`.  A negative index goes the same way, and there the
// original's conversion is one too high - `-1` becomes 0 - so the golden
// records what a defect answers rather than what a rule says.

data t { 11 22 33 }

data gaps { 5 ? 6 }

data answers {
  [ index(t, 1.7) ]         // ToInt, not the boundary rule: t[1]
  [ index(t, 2.9) ]         // still floor: t[2]
  [ index(t, 5) ]           // out of range, and no diagnostic
  [ index(t, 0 - 1) ]       // ToInt lands one too high, so t[0]
  [ size(gaps) ]            // `?` is a byte of the raw table
  [ index(gaps, 1) ]        // and the byte is zero
}

main {
  a = answers
  return
}
